import{_ as t,o as a,c as r,ak as o}from"./chunks/framework.B8jO0odJ.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Chainy local database reference","description":"Historical snapshot preserved November 11, 2026 before the technical-document cleanup.\\nUse the current document for the active design.","frontmatter":{"title":"Chainy local database reference","description":"Historical snapshot preserved November 11, 2026 before the technical-document cleanup.\\nUse the current document for the active design.","source":"docs/archive/pre-lesson-collection-2026-11-11/design/database-schema.md","category":"Archive","sourceUpdated":"2026-11-11T12:24:37+08:00","outline":[2,3]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"content/docs/archive/pre-lesson-collection-2026-11-11/design/database-schema.md","filePath":"content/docs/archive/pre-lesson-collection-2026-11-11/design/database-schema.md"}'),s={name:"content/docs/archive/pre-lesson-collection-2026-11-11/design/database-schema.md"};function i(n,e,d,c,l,p){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o(`<div><blockquote><p>Historical snapshot preserved November 11, 2026 before the technical-document cleanup. Use the <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/design/database-schema.html">current document</a> for the active design.</p></blockquote><h1 id="chainy-local-database-reference">Chainy local database reference<a class="header-anchor" href="#chainy-local-database-reference" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h1><p>Updated November 11, 2026 for the <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/game-design-lesson-collection.html">lesson-collection GDD</a>. Christian owns technical schema decisions; Sean records the agreed contract. <strong>Development gate: documentation/setup only until the owner&#39;s explicit FINAL approval.</strong> No schema, migration, Unity code, or database change is authorized by this document.</p><h2 id="current-progress-contract">Current progress contract<a class="header-anchor" href="#current-progress-contract" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>SQLite remains the local store, accessed through the existing <code>IDatabaseSystem</code> and its queued transaction boundary. The November 11 audit found that infrastructure, but it did not establish an implemented 39-lesson progression schema or tested Android persistence. The fields below are proposed logical records, not approved SQL table definitions. Prefer the smallest compatible additive extension after inspecting the actual schema during authorized development; do not replace or erase existing learner records.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Required meaning and invariant</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Profile and versions</td><td>Separate local profile ID plus supported schema/content versions; no authenticated identity or cross-device recovery is implied</td></tr><tr><td>World progress</td><td>Per-profile/world tutorial completion and the next lesson in the fixed 13-entry sequence; only Web3 is initially unlocked</td></tr><tr><td>Recorded lessons / encyclopedia</td><td>Stable lesson IDs identify completed entries; the displayed encyclopedia and collection count use the same authoritative completion set</td></tr><tr><td>Current encounter</td><td>Current minigame lesson ID and prerequisite step: 0 = item needed, 1 = item recorded/NPC needed, 2 = NPC completed/facility may launch</td></tr><tr><td>Quiz attempt</td><td>Unique attempt identity, profile, world, quiz-content version, ordered five question IDs, and active/abandoned/completed status</td></tr><tr><td>Quiz responses</td><td>First selected answer and its correctness per question, plus corrected resolution; later correction never rewrites first-response performance</td></tr><tr><td>Required review completion</td><td>A successful completed review remains recorded; world completion and next-world access derive from that committed result plus all 13 lesson entries</td></tr><tr><td>Return checkpoint</td><td>Originating world/lesson and a safe island return location identifier; no promise of mid-round or exact animation restoration</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Proposed local demo behavior is a durable reserved guest profile, such as ID 0, isolated from other nonnegative profile IDs. The audited encyclopedia adapter previously treated guest IDs as memory-only, so durable guest progress is a future integration requirement rather than an existing guarantee. Confirm the mapping to existing profiles before implementation; never merge different people merely because they share a display name or code.</p><h2 id="ordered-transitions-and-atomic-saves">Ordered transitions and atomic saves<a class="header-anchor" href="#ordered-transitions-and-atomic-saves" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Only the current lesson may advance, and its world&#39;s tutorial must already be complete. The GDD identifies which lessons require minigame success; collecting an object cannot substitute for that outcome. For a current minigame, step 0 advances after its item interaction and step 1 after its NPC interaction. At step 2 the facility may launch the activity; there is no additional persistent “scene loaded” completion step. An earlier-step retry is harmless, a future step is rejected, and callbacks for a different lesson/profile are rejected. Exit or scene-load failure preserves prerequisite steps without awarding the lesson.</p><p>Commit successful lesson completion, its encyclopedia entry, next objective, and reset of the encounter step together. Ordinary lessons and completed collections have no active encounter step. Collection completion means exactly the world&#39;s 13 required unique IDs are recorded, not that the player has interacted 13 times. It does not unlock the next world until the required review finishes.</p><p>A wrong quiz response retains the current question for correction and preserves the first response. All five fixed questions must be resolved before Finish can commit required-review completion and next-world access atomically. Store the completed attempt&#39;s first-response score out of five separately from assisted completion; a corrected five-of-five is not a perfect first-response score. Leaving or losing an unfinished session preserves its partial responses as an abandoned attempt; return starts all five questions in a new attempt, consistent with the GDD&#39;s restart rule. Replay creates a new attempt without revoking previous completion or unlocks.</p><p>Publish the new read-only progress snapshot only after commit. Retrying the same save must not duplicate entries, count another first response, or grant repeated unlocks. Serialize commands and validate the latest persisted profile/world/attempt state within the transaction; do not rely only on an earlier UI snapshot. Use the transaction&#39;s own synchronous read/write operations inside the existing database callback, without queuing nested bridge calls.</p><h2 id="validation-and-recovery-before-implementation">Validation and recovery before implementation<a class="header-anchor" href="#validation-and-recovery-before-implementation" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Reject unknown worlds, invalid lesson/question IDs, unsupported versions, impossible prerequisite stages, duplicate completion IDs, and inconsistent sequence/unlock states. Preserve corrupt or unsupported data and show a recovery route; do not overwrite it with defaults. Keep the previous checkpoint on failed saves and retain a visible retry path. The initial schema plan must explain existing-save compatibility; old V2 progress does not automatically grant new collection entries.</p><p>After FINAL approval, verification must cover two synthetic profiles, guest persistence, restart after each checkpoint, out-of-order interactions, duplicate/racing commands, failed writes, interrupted scene return, abandoned/replayed quizzes, and incompatible-version recovery. Record the named revision/build, device, expected and actual results; a static schema inspection is not runtime evidence. No economy balance, purchase, trading ledger, teacher server, or automatic cloud synchronization is needed for the current loop. See <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/archive/pre-lesson-collection-2026-11-11/technical-design.html">technical responsibilities</a> and <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/archive/pre-lesson-collection-2026-11-11/offline-data-plan.html">offline evidence boundaries</a>.</p><h2 id="historical-database-references">Historical database references<a class="header-anchor" href="#historical-database-references" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Everything below preserves the November 7 source snapshot and older source/V2 requirements. The eight-table mapping and reported defects are dated observations, not a current APK inspection; old economy fields, tasks, and migration instructions are superseded by the current contract and development gate above.</p><p>Owner: Christian Jude Villaber. Updated November 7, 2026 with a static snapshot of the local Unity checkout. Christian still owns schema decisions and implementation.</p><p>SQLite stores data on the device. This reference records decisions and source observations; it does not apply a migration to the running game. The previous generated learners/sessions/attempts/export-table schema has been <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/archive/pre-2026-10-12/docs/design/local-schema.html">archived</a>. It must not be applied as the current design.</p><h3 id="november-7-implemented-mapping--local-source-snapshot">November 7 implemented mapping — local source snapshot<a class="header-anchor" href="#november-7-implemented-mapping--local-source-snapshot" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p><code>ChainyApp/Assets/Scripts/Features/DatabaseSystem/DBSchema.cs</code> in the separate local game checkout maps eight tables. This is the implemented <em>source mapping</em> seen in a static review, not a schema dump from an installed APK or a tested migration. The configured database file is <code>chainyApp.db</code> under <code>Application.persistentDataPath</code>.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>Implemented fields</th><th>Current use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>User</code></td><td><code>user_id</code>, <code>username</code>, <code>password_hash</code>, <code>age</code>, <code>created_at</code></td><td>Local account; unique username; no learner code or teacher role field</td></tr><tr><td><code>Module</code></td><td><code>module_id</code>, <code>module_name</code>, <code>description</code></td><td>Authored module; unique name</td></tr><tr><td><code>Lesson</code></td><td><code>lesson_id</code>, <code>module_id</code>, <code>lesson_name</code></td><td>Authored lesson</td></tr><tr><td><code>Lesson_Progress</code></td><td><code>lesson_progress_id</code>, <code>user_id</code>, <code>lesson_id</code>, <code>is_completed</code>, <code>completed_at</code></td><td>Unique user/lesson pair; no score or attempts</td></tr><tr><td><code>Minigame</code></td><td><code>minigame_id</code>, <code>lesson_id</code>, <code>minigame_name</code>, <code>minigame_type</code>, <code>description</code></td><td>Authored minigame</td></tr><tr><td><code>minigame_progress</code></td><td><code>minigame_progress_id</code>, <code>user_id</code>, <code>minigame_id</code>, <code>best_score</code>, <code>is_complete</code>, <code>completed_at</code></td><td>Unique user/minigame pair</td></tr><tr><td><code>Quest</code></td><td><code>quest_id</code>, <code>module_id</code>, <code>quest_name</code>, <code>description</code></td><td>Authored quest</td></tr><tr><td><code>Quest_Progress</code></td><td><code>quest_progress_id</code>, <code>user_id</code>, <code>quest_id</code>, <code>quest_state</code>, <code>completed_at</code></td><td>Unique user/quest pair</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The current serialized configuration has three modules, six lessons, no minigames and one quest. The relationship IDs are indexed fields, not declared foreign keys. No explicit schema version or migration path was found. The source has a generic transaction API, but no wallet, inventory, reward receipt, purchase/fee ledger, restoration, encyclopedia checkpoint or versioned recovery model/service was found in the inspected first-party scripts. This does not establish what is in another revision or APK.</p><p>The static review also identified a missing update path for an existing incomplete lesson row, profile state that is loaded at <code>Start()</code> rather than reset on profile switch, and account flows that may wait indefinitely after database initialization failure. The test README references an absent <code>.csproj</code>, and its zero-quest expectation conflicts with the current configuration. Christian should verify/fix these against a named build and two synthetic profiles before acceptance. No Unity or Android runtime pass was observed in the review.</p><h3 id="v2-persistence-requirements--october-23-2026">V2 persistence requirements — October 23, 2026<a class="header-anchor" href="#v2-persistence-requirements--october-23-2026" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/archive/pre-lesson-collection-2026-11-11/game-design.html">canonical GDD</a> adds requirements not represented by the five-entity source dictionary below. Persist profile ID; build/content/schema versions; quest state and reward receipts; encyclopedia progress; inventory and balances; the shared lantern transaction/ledger; restoration flags; and activity checkpoints. AI training/practice/first-final-test state is needed only if the stretch is admitted.</p><p>Treat these as required record semantics, not approved SQL table names. Christian must inspect the actual schema, design the smallest compatible extension and test recovery before any migration. A purchase/reward must atomically update balance, ownership, ledger and quest progress; command/save retries must not repeat the economic effect. W03 expects reward 12, price 8 plus fee 1 and remaining balance 3. W04 equip is free.</p><p>The November 10 beta uses local demo profiles. Full teacher activation, protected roles and offline pairing are later service contracts; the old isTeacher flag does not implement them.</p><h3 id="october-12-source-model-reference-not-a-complete-v2-schema">October 12 source model (reference, not a complete v2 schema)<a class="header-anchor" href="#october-12-source-model-reference-not-a-complete-v2-schema" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><table><thead><tr><th>Entity</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Fields shown in the supplied dictionary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>User</td><td>Local learner/staff profile</td><td>user_id, student_code, username, age, isTeacher</td></tr><tr><td>Module</td><td>Group of learning content</td><td>module_id, module_name, description</td></tr><tr><td>Lesson</td><td>Lesson in a module</td><td>lesson_id, module_id, a truncated sequence field, a truncated lesson-title field</td></tr><tr><td>User_Progress</td><td>Current progress for a user and lesson</td><td>progress_id, user_id, lesson_id, score, attempts, is_completed, percentage</td></tr><tr><td>User_Analytics</td><td>Reported analysis records linked to progress</td><td>analytics_id, user_id, lesson_id, score, attempts, percentage</td></tr></tbody></table><p>User and Lesson relate to User_Progress; Module contains Lessons. User_Analytics is shown connected to progress, but the source does not supply a reliable implemented key design.</p><figure class="diagram"><div class="mermaid-source"><pre>erDiagram
  User ||--o{ User_Progress : has
  Module ||--o{ Lesson : contains
  Lesson ||--o{ User_Progress : tracks
</pre></div><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><button class="diagram-fullscreen">Full screen ↗</button><button class="diagram-expand" aria-expanded="false">Expand diagram</button><a href="#" class="diagram-toggle">Show source</a></figcaption></figure><p>The diagram deliberately shows only relationships clearly described in the source. Christian must confirm the analytics relationship from the actual schema.</p><h3 id="field-dictionary">Field dictionary<a class="header-anchor" href="#field-dictionary" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><table><thead><tr><th>Field(s)</th><th>Source declaration</th><th>Meaning / unresolved detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>User.user_id</td><td>Type not shown; “likely INTEGER,” presumed primary key</td><td>Actual type and primary-key definition must come from the game schema</td></tr><tr><td>User.student_code</td><td>INTEGER</td><td>Conflicts with Part VI&#39;s proposed alphanumeric input validation</td></tr><tr><td>User.username</td><td>VARCHAR(30)</td><td>Source calls it name or login name; decide whether this is a nickname and whether it is needed</td></tr><tr><td>User.age</td><td>INTEGER</td><td>Confirm research need and permitted range with Sean; no birth date is requested</td></tr><tr><td>User.isTeacher</td><td>BOOLEAN</td><td>Local role marker, not proof of staff identity or access authorization</td></tr><tr><td>Module.module_id</td><td>INTEGER primary key</td><td>Module identifier</td></tr><tr><td>Module.module_name / description</td><td>VARCHAR(255) / TEXT(65535)</td><td>Name and explanation</td></tr><tr><td>Lesson.lesson_id / module_id</td><td>INTEGER primary key / foreign key</td><td>Lesson identity and owning module</td></tr><tr><td>Lesson.sequence field / title field</td><td>INTEGER / VARCHAR(255); names truncated in source</td><td>Do not turn guessed spellings into a migration</td></tr><tr><td>User_Progress.progress_id</td><td>INTEGER primary key</td><td>Progress row identifier</td></tr><tr><td>User_Progress.user_id / lesson_id</td><td>INTEGER foreign keys</td><td>Existing user and lesson</td></tr><tr><td>User_Progress.score / attempts / percentage</td><td>INTEGER</td><td>Latest/best/cumulative score, attempt unit and percentage formula unspecified</td></tr><tr><td>User_Progress.is_completed</td><td>BOOLEAN</td><td>Completion rule/default unspecified</td></tr><tr><td>User_Analytics.analytics_id</td><td>INTEGER primary key</td><td>Analysis row identifier</td></tr><tr><td>User_Analytics remaining fields</td><td>INTEGER; linked to progress fields in the source</td><td>Snapshot, summary or derived report is not defined</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="decisions-before-schema-changes">Decisions before schema changes<a class="header-anchor" href="#decisions-before-schema-changes" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>Christian resolves these on CH-04, with Sean reviewing evaluation needs:</p><ol><li>Export the actual table definitions and confirm full names, types, keys and schema version.</li><li>Choose one learner-code format. Recommendation: TEXT if codes allow letters or meaningful leading zeroes. Do not silently convert existing records.</li><li>Define one score rule and one meaning for attempts; state the percentage denominator and rounding. A maximum score is mentioned in validation but not defined in the dictionary.</li><li>Enforce one current progress row per user/lesson if that is the intended model. Decide whether earlier attempts must be retained separately for the approved study.</li><li>Define User_Analytics. Recommendation: derive reports from authoritative records where possible instead of copying mutable scores into a second table. A historical snapshot needs an explicit identifier and meaning.</li><li>Identify the fields that support the promised session/checkpoint behavior. Decide a safe resume point; avoid saving transient animation state.</li><li>Confirm whether username/age are necessary. Keep identity maps and raw learner data outside repositories and issues.</li></ol><p>These recommendations do not override the Tech Lead&#39;s schema. The supplied dictionary is not detailed enough to safely manufacture SQL or a migration.</p><h3 id="required-validation-and-save-behavior">Required validation and save behavior<a class="header-anchor" href="#required-validation-and-save-behavior" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>The source requires unique non-null keys, valid parent relationships, nonblank required text, valid ages/Booleans, ordered lessons, unique current progress and bounded scores. Christian must implement these in the actual schema/application.</p><p>SQLite does not enforce the length in VARCHAR(30), and Boolean values use integer storage. Explicit validation is needed. See <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/datatype3.html">SQLite types</a>.</p><p>Enable and verify foreign-key enforcement on each database connection. See <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/foreignkeys.html">SQLite foreign keys</a>.</p><p>Use bound query parameters. Save related score/completion changes together, and return success only after commit; failed writes must preserve the previous save. See <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/lang_transaction.html">SQLite transactions</a>.</p><p>A repeated completion/save request must not inflate attempts or award progress twice. Christian chooses the smallest repeat-safe approach consistent with the actual schema. Do not retrofit the old event/export framework without a demonstrated need.</p><h3 id="lifecycle-and-collection">Lifecycle and collection<a class="header-anchor" href="#lifecycle-and-collection" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>Create/select profiles locally; isolate them on shared devices. A code entered on another phone does not restore records. Keep a supported checkpoint for resume, with clear feedback after interruption.</p><p>Before any real database change, record the existing version, back up consistently, test the change using synthetic data and verify that an in-place app update preserves records. This document authorizes no deletion or migration.</p><p>Teacher collection is a separate workflow in the <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/archive/pre-lesson-collection-2026-11-11/offline-data-plan.html">offline data plan</a>. SQLite storage does not upload records automatically. Research exports should contain only approved fields and enough identifiers to avoid counting the same result twice.</p><h3 id="acceptance-evidence">Acceptance evidence<a class="header-anchor" href="#acceptance-evidence" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h3><p>CH-04 needs: actual schema + two-profile isolation + result save/reload + retry without duplicate counting + failed-save recovery. CH-14 adds interrupted play and in-place update checks on the beta device. Record build, device, expected result, actual result and reviewer.</p></div>`,1)])])}const m=t(s,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
